WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
Error classification is what happens when an agentic system fails and you need to know what

2
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.880
to do next. There are three distinct error categories. Tool errors, reasoning errors,

3
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:17.280
and environment errors. Each requires a different recovery strategy. Misclassifying an error leads

4
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.159
to either retrying something that will never succeed or escalating when a simple retry

5
00:00:22.159 --> 00:00:28.559
would have worked. An agent fails and the log hands you one line of text. Where that failure

6
00:00:28.559 --> 00:00:34.959
originated decides everything you do next. A tool error means an external API or service

7
00:00:34.959 --> 00:00:41.360
returned an error, produced unexpected output, or timed out so the failure sits outside the model.

8
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.479
A reasoning error means the model produced an incorrect plan, chose the wrong tool,

9
00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:51.919
or misread a result even though it had enough information to do better so the failure sits

10
00:00:51.919 --> 00:00:58.080
inside the model. An environment error means the infrastructure underneath gave out the network,

11
00:00:58.159 --> 00:01:04.480
the database, the file system. The tool's logic is sound and the plan is sound but the environment

12
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:10.319
can't execute the step. Error classification matters because each category has a different

13
00:01:10.319 --> 00:01:17.199
correct recovery path. Tool errors may respond to a retry with back off or they may require a fall

14
00:01:17.199 --> 00:01:23.360
back to a different tool. Reasoning errors require modifying the prompt or correcting the context

15
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.480
provided to the model. A simple retry of a reasoning error just gets you the same wrong

16
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:34.080
answer again. Environment errors are often resolved by waiting and retrying after a delay.

17
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.800
Applying the wrong strategy wastes time and resources. Retrying a reasoning error over and

18
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.879
over doesn't fix anything. Escalating an environment error when a short wait would have resolved it

19
00:01:46.879 --> 00:01:52.000
creates unnecessary noise. Classification first, then recovery. That's the rule.

20
00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:58.879
Tool errors split into two subtypes and the distinction is critical. Transient tool errors

21
00:01:58.879 --> 00:02:04.639
are retryable. A network timeout, a rate limit hit, a service that's temporarily unavailable.

22
00:02:05.199 --> 00:02:11.279
The same call can succeed if you wait and try again. Permanent tool errors aren't retryable.

23
00:02:11.279 --> 00:02:17.520
Invalid input, permission denied, resource not found. The call will fail every single time

24
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.919
regardless of how many times you retry it. The HTTP status code often signals the category.

25
00:02:23.919 --> 00:02:30.800
500 level errors are usually transient.400 level errors are usually permanent. The exam trap is

26
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.960
straightforward. Retrying a permanent tool error wastes tokens and delays getting to a fallback

27
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:42.720
or escalation path that might actually work. Acting on that split is a separate design problem.

28
00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.880
Back off, retry budgets, win to abort, and it gets a lesson of its own. For now, what matters

29
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:54.880
is being able to tell the two apart. Three of the most common transient tool errors are worth

30
00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.720
naming explicitly. A network timeout means the API didn't respond within the allowed window.

31
00:03:01.279 --> 00:03:06.559
It's usually worth retrying with a short wait. A rate limit means you sent too many requests

32
00:03:06.559 --> 00:03:12.559
in a given window. The correct response is to wait for the back off period the service specifies,

33
00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:18.399
then retry. Temporary unavailability means the service itself is momentarily down.

34
00:03:18.399 --> 00:03:23.360
Exponential back off with a maximum retry count is the standard recovery pattern here.

35
00:03:23.919 --> 00:03:29.600
All three of these share the same trait. The failure is circumstantial, not structural.

36
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:35.520
The same request sent again after a delay can succeed. That's what makes them transient and

37
00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:40.320
distinguishes them from permanent errors that require a fundamentally different response.

38
00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.399
Reasoning errors are the category where the model itself is the failure point.

39
00:03:46.399 --> 00:03:51.679
The tool ran correctly. The infrastructure was available, but the model produced an incorrect

40
00:03:51.679 --> 00:03:58.080
plan, selected the wrong tool, or misread a result. The key characteristic, retrying with

41
00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:04.000
identical input, will produce the same error. The model state hasn't changed. Its context is the

42
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.919
same. It will make the same mistake again. Recovery requires changing something about the input,

43
00:04:09.919 --> 00:04:15.440
modifying the prompt, adding missing context, correcting a hallucinated assumption,

44
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:21.760
or escalating to a human when the model can't self-correct. Common causes include ambiguous

45
00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.200
instructions, missing context about the current state of the world, and the model filling gaps

46
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:33.200
in its context with plausible sounding but incorrect information. Environment errors

47
00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:39.040
occupy the layer below both the agent and its tools. The model's plan is correct. The tool's

48
00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:45.519
logic is correct. But the environment that the tool depends on is unavailable. A network partition,

49
00:04:45.519 --> 00:04:50.239
a database that can't be reached, a file system that returns permission errors.

50
00:04:50.239 --> 00:04:54.799
These are environment errors. They're often transient. The network comes back up. The

51
00:04:54.799 --> 00:05:00.559
database recovers. The key to classifying them correctly is asking where the failure originated.

52
00:05:00.559 --> 00:05:05.600
If the tool itself reported the error, it's a tool error. If the tool couldn't even reach its

53
00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.480
own dependencies, it's an environment error. That distinction matters because it changes

54
00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:15.920
the recovery path. Environment errors often resolve on their own. Tool errors may not.

55
00:05:17.119 --> 00:05:22.720
The classification rule is a single question. Where did the failure originate? If the tool

56
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:28.720
or API reported the failure, it's a tool error. If the tool ran but the plan or interpretation

57
00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:33.440
was wrong, it's a reasoning error. If the tool couldn't reach its own dependencies,

58
00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:39.040
it's an environment error. Each classification leads directly to a recovery strategy.

59
00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:45.839
Tool errors check for transient versus permanent, then retry or fall back accordingly. Reasoning

60
00:05:45.839 --> 00:05:53.600
errors modify the prompt or context, never just retry blindly. Environment errors wait, retry after

61
00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:59.359
a delay, and monitor for resolution. Applying this rule consistently prevents misclassification

62
00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:05.839
and the wasted effort that follows. Picture an agent hammering a retry loop against a plan that

63
00:06:05.839 --> 00:06:12.640
was wrong from the beginning. It'll be wrong the 15th time too, and you'll pay for every attempt.

64
00:06:13.279 --> 00:06:19.279
That's what misclassifying a failure can cost you. Asking where the error originated, outside the

65
00:06:19.279 --> 00:06:24.399
model, inside it, or underneath a tool, is the cheapest question in the recovery path.

66
00:06:24.959 --> 00:06:28.000
And that wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

